exports[`BYOK wire format (custom) > actionItems 1`] = `
[
  {
    "body": {
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "You are writing the FOLLOW-UPS from a finished internal working meeting, for ME. The meeting is OVER. These are the things the meeting agreed someone would go do, plus anything left open that needs chasing.

You are given the FINDINGS from the analysis (notable moments, each with an id) and the full timestamped transcript. Produce the follow-ups this meeting generated — things someone agreed to do, decisions that need writing up or communicating, and open questions that need chasing before the next session. In an internal meeting nearly everything is "us"; use "them" only for an outside party (a client, vendor, partner) who owes something. For each action:
- text: the concrete next step, phrased as an action. It must stand on its own — carry the WHAT into this line rather than leaving it to a separate "why" field, but keep it to one readable sentence.
- side: WHOSE follow-up it is. "us" = ME and my own side (my company / team — everyone on the user's side of the table); "them" = the other party. Cover BOTH sides: what my side committed to or needs to do, AND what the other party said they would do or still owes. null only when it truly belongs to neither.
- owner: the person who should do it, by the name the transcript uses (their speaker label, or a name someone said, e.g. "Kevin"), when the transcript makes it clear; else null. Never invent a name.
- linkedEventId: the finding id it derives from when it maps to one, else null.
- time: the [m:ss] it relates to (copy a real transcript timestamp), else null.

Be selective — surface the actions that genuinely matter (typically 3-7 in total across both sides), not busywork. Ground everything in what was actually said.

Return your answer strictly as a single JSON object matching the provided schema. Use the schema's property names EXACTLY (verbatim) — do not rename, translate, or add top-level keys.

Write ALL of your prose output (summaries, titles, explanations, advice) in Traditional Chinese (繁體中文), regardless of the language spoken in the transcript. Keep any VERBATIM quotes you cite from the transcript in their original language.",
          "role": "system",
        },
        {
          "content": "ABOUT ME / US (the side you are advising):
- Name: Jack
- Role: Founder
- Company / side: Pathors

FIRST work out which named speaker in the transcript is ME / US: match the name/role/company/background above, the meeting context, and HOW each speaker talks (which side's interests, asks, and concerns they voice). Everyone else is the OTHER PARTY ("them"). Then attribute every statement, question, concession, and demand to the correct side — never mistake the other party's words for mine, and never advise from THEIR side. If it is genuinely unclear who is who, infer the most likely ME from the context rather than defaulting to the wrong side.

Findings:
### id: f1
[me] Price accepted: ME accepted the price without a trade.

Full transcript:
[0:00] [Remote 1] We agreed to ship on Friday.
[0:04] [Me] Um, I think the price is fine.",
          "role": "user",
        },
      ],
      "model": "byok-smart",
      "response_format": {
        "type": "json_object",
      },
      "stream": true,
    },
    "headers": {
      "authorization": "Bearer custom-key",
      "content-type": "application/json",
    },
    "url": "https://llm.example.test/v1/chat/completions",
  },
]
`;

exports[`BYOK wire format (custom) > ask 1`] = `
[
