exports[`BYOK wire format (groq) > deliveryLive 1`] = `
[
  {
    "body": {
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "You are a delivery coach for the user in a live conversation. Judge the USER'S OWN delivery only — never the other party's; use the profile to tell which speaker is the user. Two things:
1) TONE — reserve 'aggressive'/'rude' for genuinely hostile, demeaning, dismissive, or contemptuous wording; firm disagreement or pushback is 'firm', not aggressive. When uncertain, prefer the milder label.
2) FILLER WORDS / VERBAL CRUTCHES — consider ONLY the lexical words/phrases in the provided watchlist. Do NOT consider non-lexical hesitation sounds (um, uh, er, 呃, 啊, 嗯) — speech-to-text usually drops them, so they won't be in the transcript. Filler use is normal and human: flag 'frequent' ONLY when the user leans on watchlist words as crutches densely enough to distract a listener; never flag ordinary, meaningful uses of those same words, and never flag mere presence.
Be honest and concise.

Return your answer strictly as a single JSON object matching the provided schema. Use the schema's property names EXACTLY (verbatim) — do not rename, translate, or add top-level keys.

Write ALL of your prose output (summaries, titles, explanations, advice) in Traditional Chinese (繁體中文), regardless of the language spoken in the transcript. Keep any VERBATIM quotes you cite from the transcript in their original language.",
          "role": "system",
        },
        {
          "content": "ABOUT ME / US (the side you are advising):
- Name: Jack
- Role: Founder
- Company / side: Pathors

FIRST work out which named speaker in the transcript is ME / US: match the name/role/company/background above, the meeting context, and HOW each speaker talks (which side's interests, asks, and concerns they voice). Everyone else is the OTHER PARTY ("them"). Then attribute every statement, question, concession, and demand to the correct side — never mistake the other party's words for mine, and never advise from THEIR side. If it is genuinely unclear who is who, infer the most likely ME from the context rather than defaulting to the wrong side.

Filler watchlist (judge OVER-use of these as verbal crutches only — ignore meaningful uses, and ignore non-lexical um/uh sounds): 那個, 這個, 就是, 就是說, 然後, 反正, 其實, 基本上, 怎麼講, 怎麼說, 你知道, 你知道嗎, 你懂嗎, 對不對, 對啊對啊, 這樣子, 之類的, 什麼的, 老實說, 說真的, 的部分, 的話, like, you know, i mean, basically, actually, literally, sort of, kind of, kinda, sorta, you see, i guess, right?, so yeah, to be honest, at the end of the day, or whatever, and stuff

Recent transcript:
[Remote 1] We agreed to ship on Friday.
[Me] Um, I think the price is fine.",
          "role": "user",
        },
      ],
      "model": "byok-fast",
      "response_format": {
        "json_schema": {
          "name": "response",
          "schema": {
            "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#",
            "additionalProperties": false,
            "properties": {
              "filler_examples": {
                "description": "The specific tics you actually heard the user overuse (e.g. ['就是','然後'] / ['um','like']); [] when ok.",
                "items": {
                  "type": "string",
                },
                "type": "array",
              },
              "filler_level": {
                "description": "'frequent' ONLY when filler words / verbal tics are dense enough in a short stretch to distract (e.g. many in a sentence or two). Everyone uses some fillers — never flag mere presence; default to 'ok'.",
                "enum": [
                  "ok",
                  "frequent",
                ],
                "type": "string",
              },
              "filler_note": {
                "description": "One short line about the over-frequent stretch (where/what); empty when level is 'ok'.",
                "type": "string",
              },
              "pace": {
                "description": "How fast the user is speaking overall.",
                "enum": [
                  "slow",
                  "comfortable",
                  "fast",
                ],
                "type": "string",
              },
              "summary": {
                "description": "One short plain-language line on how the user is coming across right now.",
                "type": "string",
              },
              "tone": {
                "description": "Overall tone of YOUR OWN most recent contributions — not the other party's.",
                "enum": [
                  "neutral",
                  "warm",
                  "firm",
                  "sharp",
                  "aggressive",
                  "rude",
                ],
                "type": "string",
              },
              "tone_evidence": {
                "description": "A short verbatim quote from YOUR OWN words that shows the tone; empty if none.",
                "type": "string",
